  <h1>Architecture styles explained</h1>
  <p class="lead muted">A fast, reliable tour of the major styles &mdash; roughly chronological &mdash; so you can name what you are looking at and place your own work in context.</p>

  <p>Styles are a shorthand, not a cage. Real buildings borrow, overlap and rebel against the labels below, and the dates are approximate because movements bleed into one another. Still, knowing the defining features and a landmark example for each gives you a mental map: it lets you read precedent quickly, talk to tutors precisely, and understand why a building looks the way it does. Use the table as a reference and follow up the examples that intrigue you.</p>

  <h2>From the classical world to the contemporary</h2>
  <p>Each row gives the rough period, the ideas that drive the style, and a building worth knowing.</p>
  <table class="data">
    <tr><th>Style</th><th>Period</th><th>What defines it &amp; a key example</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Classical (Greek &amp; Roman)</td><td>c. 700 BCE &ndash; 400 CE</td><td>Order, symmetry and proportion expressed through columns (Doric, Ionic, Corinthian), pediments and the arch. Example: the Parthenon, Athens.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Gothic</td><td>c. 1140 &ndash; 1500</td><td>Height and light: pointed arches, ribbed vaults, flying buttresses and vast stained glass. Example: Chartres Cathedral, France.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Renaissance</td><td>c. 1400 &ndash; 1600</td><td>A revival of classical order, harmony and mathematical proportion. Example: Brunelleschi&rsquo;s dome, Florence Cathedral.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Baroque</td><td>c. 1600 &ndash; 1750</td><td>Drama, movement and theatricality; curved forms, rich ornament and bold plays of light and shadow. Example: St Peter&rsquo;s Square, Rome (Bernini).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Neoclassical</td><td>c. 1750 &ndash; 1850</td><td>A restrained return to classical purity and grandeur, often civic. Example: the Panth&eacute;on, Paris.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Arts &amp; Crafts</td><td>c. 1860 &ndash; 1910</td><td>A reaction against industry: honest materials, craftsmanship and vernacular form. Example: Red House, Bexleyheath (Philip Webb / William Morris).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Art Deco</td><td>c. 1920 &ndash; 1940</td><td>Glamorous, geometric and streamlined; stepped forms, bold motifs and luxurious materials. Example: the Chrysler Building, New York.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Modernism</td><td>c. 1920 &ndash; 1970</td><td>&ldquo;Form follows function&rdquo;: clean lines, open plans, glass and steel, ornament rejected. Example: Villa Savoye, Poissy (Le Corbusier).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Brutalism</td><td>c. 1950 &ndash; 1980</td><td>Raw exposed concrete (b&eacute;ton brut), massive forms and honest structure. Example: the National Theatre, London (Denys Lasdun).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Postmodernism</td><td>c. 1970 &ndash; 1990</td><td>Playful reaction to modernist austerity: colour, historical quotation, wit and symbolism. Example: the Piazza d&rsquo;Italia, New Orleans (Charles Moore).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-tech</td><td>c. 1970 &ndash; present</td><td>Structure and services celebrated as the architecture; exposed steel, ducts and bright colour. Example: the Centre Pompidou, Paris (Piano &amp; Rogers).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Deconstructivism</td><td>c. 1980 &ndash; present</td><td>Fragmentation, distortion and non-rectilinear forms that appear to defy stability. Example: the Guggenheim Museum, Bilbao (Frank Gehry).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Parametric / contemporary</td><td>c. 2000 &ndash; present</td><td>Fluid, computationally generated geometries enabled by digital tools and new fabrication. Example: the Heydar Aliyev Center, Baku (Zaha Hadid).</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>How to use styles in your own work</h2>
  <p>When you cite a precedent in a review, do not just say a scheme &ldquo;looks brutalist&rdquo;. Name the specific quality you are borrowing &mdash; the way brutalism expresses structure honestly, or the way high-tech pushes services to the edges to free the plan. Tutors respond to precise readings, not vague vibes. It also protects you from the common trap of copying a style&rsquo;s surface (say, a concrete finish) while ignoring the logic that made it meaningful in its own time and place.</p>

  <p>Remember too that most great buildings resist a single label. The best way to internalise these categories is to walk your own city with them in mind, photograph what you see, and try to argue which style each building belongs to &mdash; and where it breaks the rules.</p>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Tip.</strong> Build a personal precedent library: for every style here, save one plan, one section and one photograph. When you are stuck in design, comparing your scheme against three real precedents will unblock you faster than staring at a blank page.</div>
